      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just to understand: I see that drivers/firmware/google/gsmi.c uses GSMI_HANDSHAKE_SPIN during probe to overcome some issues when SMI are not kind of synchronous and added a spin loop. Do I correctly understand this commit log part \"So probe GSMI support during pKVM initialization, similarly to how the\nGSMI kernel driver in drivers/firmware/google/gsmi.c does that (though\na bit differently, as we require the GSMI_HANDSHAKE_NONE protocol, since\nthat is the hanshake protocol that is implemented by modern versions of\ncoreboot and thus relevant on modern Chromebook platforms).\" as: on our modern chips we do not have to use GSMI_HANDSHAKE_SPIN as GSMI_HANDSHAKE_NONE is guaranteed? If so perhaps adding comment here like:\n```\n\t/*\n\t * Modern versions of coreboot implement GSMI_HANDSHAKE_NONE,\n\t * as SMI execution is synchronous on modern Chromebook platforms,\n\t * meaning we do not have to use GSMI_HANDSHAKE_SPIN to poll for\n\t * completion.\n\t */\n```\nwould be helpful?",
